onth>10</month><volume>29</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage>933</fpage><lpage>940</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18667943</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Intraoperative findings and types of ossiculoplasty in attic cholesteatomas according to the extent</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ceo-2-39-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>SS: stapes suprastructure; PORP: partial ossicular replacement prosthesis; TORP: total ossicular replacement prosthesis.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Changes in hearing levels in patients with an attic cholesteatoma</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ceo-2-39-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>The average air-bone gap improved postoperatively. The bone conduction level was unchanged after surgery in all the patients. <sup>*</sup><italic>P</italic>&#x0003c;0.05.</p><p>AB gap: air-bone gap (0.5, 1, 2, and 3 kHz); BC: bone conduction (1, 2, and 4 kHz).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T3" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Changes in hearing levels in the patients undergoing attic reconstruction or attic obliteration</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ceo-2-39-i003"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>In the attic obliteration group, the average postoperative air-bone gap was not different from the preoperative hearing and the average gap improved in the patients with attic reconstruction. Bone conduction did not deteriorate in either group. <sup>*</sup><italic>P</italic>&#x0003c;0.05.</p><p>AB gap: air-bone gap (0.5, 1, 2, and 3 kHz); BC: bone conduction (1, 2, and 4 kHz).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>